00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:11.459 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it is a common misconception that the  builders of the pyramids were slaves  forced to work by the king however these  workers were for the most part skilled  builders and craftsmen in the permanent  service of the king the specific numbers  of the workforce needed for the building  of such monuments is difficult to  precisely identify what we do know is  that these men were divided into crews  of peasant conscripts of about 2,000 men  the crews were divided into two gangs  which in turn were divided into five  groups called Saha of 200 men these  groups were themselves divided into ten  divisions of 20 men or maybe 20  divisions of 10 men the different gangs  had names which usually included that of  the reigning king such as Friends of  Khufu and the drunkards of men Caray  these different gangs had different  tasks within the construction of the  pyramid  thanks to expeditions carried out  throughout the 20th century we have  access to the skeletal remains of some  of the pyramid builders and these have  been analyzed in bio archaeological  studies to be able to further gauge how  the health of the pyramid builders  compared to other people living at Giza  contemporaneously various indicators of  health have been compared from these two  populations coming from the western  cemetery for high officials and the  southeast cemetery for workers the  evidence shows that the pyramid workers  died earlier in life than their more  privileged counterparts who were also  living at Giza men died nearly 10 years  earlier and women died roughly 5 years  sooner the female workers from Giza  however exhibit a smaller stature than  their Amarna counterparts standing at  150 point 5 3 centimeters while the  Amarna women were roughly 154  zero two centimeters the laborers at  Giza displays severe changes to their  vertebral bodies the largest part of an  individual vertebra which indicates that  they experienced fairly high levels of  spinal trauma recent evidence from the  Giza Plateau suggests that at least in  the case of Khafre and Menkaure  the workmen were living in permanent  settlements that actually represent some  of the earliest known urban planning in  these workman's villages we find  abundant remains of high quality animals  such as cattle and sheep and goats which  were not normally part of the daily diet  of the commoners in addition the remains  of two bakeries and one hospital were  recovered again pointing to the  high-quality treatment that these  workers received  you

